==> CPU frequency (driver none)
  good: cores ramp to full turbo the moment a request arrives: EPP performance or balance_performance, turbo on, no frequency caps — token generation is a latency-bound loop and every ramp-up shows in time-to-first-token
  src:  https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/pm/intel_pstate.html#energy-vs-performance-hints, https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/pm/cpufreq.html
  info: no cpufreq policy exposed (VM or cpufreq disabled) — nothing to tune here
        from: /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/
==> Isolation for sandboxed agents
  good: /dev/kvm usable for microVM sandboxes, each GPU alone in its IOMMU group, split-lock detection on, CET known, GPU observation streams limited to CAP_PERFMON
  src:  https://docs.kernel.org/driver-api/vfio.html (IOMMU groups), https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/kernel-parameters.html (split_lock_detect), https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/sysctl/kernel.html (split_lock_mitigate), kernel source drivers/gpu/drm/i915/i915_perf.c and drivers/gpu/drm/xe/xe_observation.c (paranoid sysctls), Intel SDM vol. 4 335592-093 (IA32_S_CET 6A2H, IA32_TME_ACTIVATE 982H)
  note: the CPU has VT-x (vmx) but /dev/kvm is missing: no microVM sandboxes
        from:   /proc/cpuinfo (vmx), /dev/kvm
        why:    kvm_intel creates /dev/kvm; without it Kata or Firecracker cannot start a VM
        apply:  sudo modprobe kvm_intel
        revert: sudo modprobe -r kvm_intel
  note: no IOMMU unit registered: no DMA isolation between devices, no GPU passthrough into a microVM
        from:   /sys/class/iommu/
        why:    VFIO hands a device to a VM only with its IOMMU group; without VT-d enabled there are no groups
        apply:  BIOS: enable VT-d, then add intel_iommu=on to the kernel command line and reboot
        revert: remove intel_iommu=on from the kernel command line and reboot
  info: CET flags: ibt
        from: /proc/cpuinfo

==> 2 suggestion(s). Read-only: nothing on this host was changed.
exit=0
